## Why This Is Asked
This question introduces a new DP angle: partitioning a string into k segments with a per-segment distinct-character score, a practical proxy for text analytics. It tests state formulation, precomputation, and reconstruction, with a baseline O(n^2k) DP suitable for beginner to intermediate levels.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitioning problems
- Precomputing substring properties in O(n^2)
- Reconstructing cuts via parent pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxScorePartition(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    const seen = new Set();
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      seen.add(s[j]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction could be added here
  return {score: dp[n][k], cutStartIndices: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) and compute dist on the fly?
- How does the approach change if k can be 0 or n+? and if n is large?

## Why This Is Asked

This question introduces a non-linear block value that combines sum and minimum, forcing a DP that accounts for subarray metrics and partition points. It also tests reconstruction of exact k segments and handling of large intermediate data.

## Key Concepts

- Non-linear block cost: (sum) * (min)
- Precomputation of all subarray metrics V[l][r]
- DP over prefixes with exactly k blocks
- Path reconstruction from predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space (reducible to O(nk))

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const V = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = Infinity;
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      const sum = pref[r+1]-pref[l];
      V[l][r] = sum * mn;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0] = NEG;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      pre[i][t] = pre[i-1][t];
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + V[j][i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; pre[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while(t>0){
    const j = pre[i][t];
    blocks.push([j, i-1]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reduce memory to O(nk) with a streaming recomputation of V? 
- How would the approach adapt if k is large and n up to 2e5 (any pruning or heuristic)?

## Why This Is Asked
Models practical scheduling under capacity with a convex (squared) day cost, exercising 2D DP and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and days
- Transition constrained by daily capacity D
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Backtracking via parent pointers to recover daily blocks
- Complexity: O(n^2 m) time, O(n m) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSchedule(A, m, D) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(m + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let d = 1; d <= m; d++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const sum = pref[i] - pref[j];
        if (sum <= D && dp[j][d - 1] !== INF) {
          const cost = dp[j][d - 1] + sum * sum;
          if (cost < dp[i][d]) {
            dp[i][d] = cost;
            par[i][d] = j;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  if (dp[n][m] === INF) return { cost: -1, blocks: [] };
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, d = m;
  while (d > 0) {
    const j = par[i][d];
    blocks.push([j + 1, i]);
    i = j;
    d--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { cost: dp[n][m], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to near O(n m) if D is small or sums have tighter bounds?
- How would you present the actual day blocks to the user, including start/end indices and sums?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests fixed-length path DP and path reconstruction in graphs, a core DP pattern that shows depth beyond standard knapsack.

## Key Concepts
- DP on path length t
- State transition using edges
- Backpointer reconstruction
- Time and space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEdgePath(n, edges, k) {
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) dp[v][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (const e of edges) {
      const {u,v,w} = e;
      const prev = dp[u][t-1];
      if (prev !== NEG) {
        const cand = prev + w;
        if (cand > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = cand;
          par[v][t] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let bestV = 0, best = NEG;
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) if (dp[v][k] > best) { best = dp[v][k]; bestV = v; }
  const path = [];
  let cur = bestV;
  for (let t=k; t>=1; t--) { path.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; }
  path.push(cur);
  path.reverse();
  return {path, weight: best};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this change if edges could have negative weights? How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to model non-crossing, higher-arity structures with DP and reconstruct a solution.

## Key Concepts

- DP state dp[i][t] for i and t
- Non-crossing triple formation
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Time/space trade-offs and optimization path

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTriples(A, n, k) {
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) {
        for (let i1=1; i1<i; i1++) {
          for (let j=i1+1; j<i; j++) {
            const prev = (i1>=2 ? dp[i1-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : -Infinity));
            const val = prev + A[i1-1]*A[j-1]*A[i-1];
            if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = {i1, j}; }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction logic would backtrack using prv
  return {value: dp[n][k], triples: []};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests a concrete DP pattern for selecting exactly k non-overlapping subarrays with a fixed per-subarray cost. It requires careful state definitions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling of negative sums.

## Key Concepts

- DP for k disjoint subarrays with end-state tracking
- Prefix sums or running sums to compute subarray value fast
- Reconstruction pointers to recover endpoints
- Time: O(nk); Space: O(nk) (with optimizations)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length, NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = NEG;
      if (t > 0) end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How handle cases where all values are negative or c is large?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design with an extra edit dimension on a classic string problem; requires careful transitions for matches, edits, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings (i, j)
- Extra dimension for edits (t)
- Transitions for match/mismatch with edits
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxLPSWithEdits(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[i][i][t] = 1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++) {
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++) {
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
        let best = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t]);
        if (s[i] === s[j]) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t] + 2);
        if (t >= 1) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-1] + 2);
        if (t >= 2) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-2] + 2);
        dp[i][j][t] = best;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to require exactly k edits instead of at most?
- How to reduce space to O(n*k)?
- How to reconstruct the edited string and the subsequence?

## Why This Is Asked
- Tests DP state design that tracks both position and budget (flips).
- Requires reconstruction to return exact indices, not just value.

## Key Concepts
- DP state with dual dimension (i, flips)
- Transition depends on current bit; reconstruction via predecessor pointers
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOnesRun(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: k+1}, () => null));
  let bestLen = 0, bestEnd = 0, bestJ = 0;
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    for (let j=0; j<=k; j++) {
      if (s[i] === '1') {
        dp[i][j] = (i>0 ? dp[i-1][j] : 0) + 1;
        prev[i][j] = i>0 ? [i-1, j] : null;
      } else if (j>0) {
        dp[i][j] = (i>0 ? dp[i-1][j-1] : 0) + 1;
        prev[i][j] = i>0 ? [i-1, j-1] : null;
      } else {
        dp[i][j] = 0; prev[i][j] = null;
      }
      if (dp[i][j] > bestLen) { bestLen = dp[i][j]; bestEnd = i; bestJ = j; }
    }
  }
  const start = bestEnd - bestLen + 2;
  return {bestLen, start, end: bestEnd+1, flips: bestJ};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce space to O(k)?
- How would you adapt if flips must happen in blocks?

